## Summary

The bug was in `src/utils/deepEqual.ts`. When comparing an empty array `[]` with an empty plain object `{}`, both have zero keys, so the function fell through to the (empty) key-comparison loop and returned `true`.

I added a targeted check right after the key-length comparison:

```ts
if (
  keys1.length === 0 &&
  ((Array.isArray(object1) && isPlainObject(object2)) ||
    (isPlainObject(object1) && Array.isArray(object2)))
) {
  return false;
}
```

This returns `false` when one value is an empty array and the other is an empty plain object, in either argument order. Because the function recurses into nested values, it also handles the nested cases (`{ items: [] }` vs `{ items: {} }` and vice versa).

Importantly, the check is gated on `keys1.length === 0`, so it does **not** affect the existing behavior where a non-empty sparse array with numeric-string keys compares equal to a plain object with matching keys (`deepEqual(sparseArray, plainObject) === true`). I verified all cases — including that existing contract, arrays vs arrays, and objects vs objects — behave correctly.

I did not touch anything in the tests directory.
